= 1E
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Linear Algebra}

= Solution
{parent=1E}

For $n\geq1$, an <eigenvalue> is a number $\lambda\in\mathbb C$ for which there is a nonzero <vector> $v$ satisfying $Av=\lambda v$. Equivalently, $\ker(A-\lambda I)\ne\{0\}$, or $\det(A-\lambda I)=0$. The <characteristic polynomial> $\det(tI-A)$ has degree $n$ and leading coefficient one. By the <fundamental theorem of algebra> it has a complex root $\lambda$. At that root $A-\lambda I$ is a <singular matrix>, so it has a nonzero <kernel> <vector>, proving that $A$ has an <eigenvalue>.

= i
{parent=1e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

**True.** If $Av=\lambda v$ with $v\ne0$ and $A=A^*$, then
$$
\lambda v^*v=v^*Av=(v^*Av)^*=\bar\lambda v^*v.
$$
Since $v^*v>0$, $\lambda=\bar\lambda$. Thus every <eigenvalue> of a <Hermitian matrix> is real.

= ii
{parent=1e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

**False.** The <matrix> $A=\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\0&2\end{pmatrix}$ has real <eigenvalues> $1$ and $2$, because its <characteristic polynomial> is $(t-1)(t-2)$, but $A^*=\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\1&2\end{pmatrix}\ne A$. Real spectrum alone does not imply that it is a <Hermitian matrix>.

= iii
{parent=1e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

**False.** Every entry of $A=\begin{pmatrix}1&2\\2&1\end{pmatrix}$ is strictly positive, yet $A(1,-1)^T=-(1,-1)^T$. Its <eigenvalues> are $3$ and $-1$, so one has negative real part. Positivity of entries does not imply the <positive-definite matrix> property.

= iv
{parent=1e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

**False in general.** Take $A=\operatorname{diag}(1,6,6)$ and $B=\operatorname{diag}(2,2,9)$. Both have <trace> $13$ and <determinant> $36$, but their <eigenvalue> multisets are different. For $n>3$, append equal identity blocks to preserve this counterexample. In dimensions one and two the assertion is true: in dimension two the <characteristic polynomial> is $t^2-(\operatorname{tr}A)t+\det A$, whose roots, with multiplicity, are determined by those two quantities.

= 2G
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Geometry}

= Solution
{parent=2G}

Let $Q\in\mathrm{SO}(3,\mathbb R)$. Since $Q^TQ=I$ and $\det Q=1$,
$$
\det(Q-I)=\det Q\,\det(I-Q^T)=\det(I-Q)=-\det(Q-I),
$$
so $\det(Q-I)=0$. Choose a <unit vector> $e$ with $Qe=e$. Its <orthogonal complement> is an <invariant subspace>: for $v\perp e$, $e\cdot Qv=(Q^Te)\cdot v=e\cdot v=0$. In an <orthonormal basis> $(e,e_1,e_2)$, $Q$ therefore has a scalar block $1$ and a two-dimensional <orthogonal matrix> block of <determinant> one. Such a block has columns $(\cos\theta,\sin\theta)^T$ and $(-\sin\theta,\cos\theta)^T$. Consequently $Q$ is a <rotation in three dimensions> about the axis $\mathbb Re$ through angle $\theta$, including the identity when $\theta=0$.

To construct the <Householder reflections>, let $S_0$ reflect in the plane spanned by $e,e_1$ and let $S_\psi$ reflect in the plane spanned by $e,\cos\psi\,e_1+\sin\psi\,e_2$. They fix $e$, while on $e^\perp$ their <matrices> are
$$
S_0=\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\0&-1\end{pmatrix},\qquad S_\psi=\begin{pmatrix}\cos2\psi&\sin2\psi\\\sin2\psi&-\cos2\psi\end{pmatrix}.
$$
Their product is the planar <rotation matrix> through $2\psi$. Taking $\psi=\theta/2$ proves $\boxed{Q=S_{\theta/2}S_0}$. This gives the <composition of two plane reflections> explicitly; each is a <Householder reflection> $I-2nn^T$ for the corresponding unit plane normal $n$.

= 3C
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Complex Analysis or Complex Methods}

= a
{parent=3c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For a regular smooth curve $z=z_j(t)$ passing through $z_0$, the complex tangent of its image is $dw_j/dt=f'(z_0)z_j'(t)$. When $f'(z_0)\ne0$, both tangents are multiplied by the same nonzero complex number. Multiplication rescales lengths by $|f'(z_0)|$ and rotates every direction by $\arg f'(z_0)$, so the difference of tangent arguments is unchanged. Hence the <analytic function> defines a <conformal map> at $z_0$: **oriented angles, and therefore ordinary angles between the curves, are preserved**. The nonzero derivative condition also ensures that the image tangents are nonzero.

= b
{parent=3c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Write $f=u+iv$ and $z=x+iy$. The <Cauchy-Riemann equations> give $u_x=v_y$ and $u_y=-v_x$. Thus the real <Jacobian determinant> is
$$
\boxed{\frac{\partial(u,v)}{\partial(x,y)}=u_xv_y-u_yv_x=u_x^2+v_x^2=|u_x+iv_x|^2=|f'(z)|^2.}
$$
This identity also holds at points where $f'(z)=0$; there the Jacobian is zero and local angle preservation is not guaranteed.

= 4C
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Special Relativity}

= Solution
{parent=4C}

Choose the transverse axis so that the <photon> travels in the $xy$ plane. Its energy-momentum <four-vector> is $p^\mu=(E/c,E\cos\theta/c,E\sin\theta/c,0)$. A <Lorentz boost> of speed $v$ along $x$, with $\beta=v/c$ and $\gamma=(1-\beta^2)^{-1/2}$, gives
$$
E'/c=\gamma(E/c-\beta p_x),\qquad p_x'=\gamma(p_x-\beta E/c).
$$
Because a <photon> has zero invariant mass, $|\mathbf p'|=E'/c$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{E'=\frac{E(1-\beta\cos\theta)}{\sqrt{1-\beta^2}},\qquad\cos\theta'=\frac{cp_x'}{E'}=\frac{\cos\theta-\beta}{1-\beta\cos\theta}.}
$$
The first is the <relativistic Doppler effect> in angular form, and the second is <relativistic aberration>.

= 5B
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Fluid Dynamics}

= Solution
{parent=5B}

The <divergence> is $\partial_x(e^y\sinh x)+\partial_y(-e^y\cosh x)=e^y\cosh x-e^y\cosh x=0$, proving <incompressibility>. Use the <stream function> convention $u_x=\psi_y$, $u_y=-\psi_x$. Integration of either component then gives
$$
\boxed{\psi(x,y)=e^y\sinh x+C.}
$$
The <streamlines> are its level curves. Setting the irrelevant constant to zero, a nonzero level $K$ has
$$
y=\ln|K|-\ln|\sinh x|,
$$
with $x>0$ for $K>0$ and $x<0$ for $K<0$. Each curve rises to $y=+\infty$ as $x\to0$ from its side and approaches $y=\ln(2|K|)-|x|$ at large $|x|$. The zero <streamline> is $x=0$. Flow is downward everywhere, outward to the right when $x>0$ and outward to the left when $x<0$; on $x=0$ it is vertically downward.

\Image[paper-1-streamlines.png]
{title=Streamlines and flow direction for the exponential hyperbolic flow}

= 6D
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Numerical Analysis}

= Solution
{parent=6D}

A unit lower triangular $L$ is invertible. For $x\ne0$, $y=L^Tx\ne0$, so an <LDL decomposition> with positive diagonal $D$ gives
$$
x^TAx=(L^Tx)^TD(L^Tx)=\sum_i d_i y_i^2>0.
$$
Also $A=LDL^T$ is symmetric. Hence $A$ is a <positive-definite matrix>.

For the specified <matrix>, comparing entries successively gives $d_1=1$, $l_{21}=-1$, $l_{31}=2$, $d_2=3-l_{21}^2d_1=2$, and
$$
l_{32}=\frac{1-l_{31}l_{21}d_1}{d_2}=\frac32,\qquad d_3=3-l_{31}^2d_1-l_{32}^2d_2=-\frac{11}{2}.
$$
Thus
$$
\boxed{L=\begin{pmatrix}1&0&0\\-1&1&0\\2&3/2&1\end{pmatrix},\qquad D=\operatorname{diag}(1,2,-11/2).}
$$
The last pivot is negative, so this particular factorization is outside the positive-diagonal case proved above. Indeed $x=(-2,0,1)^T$ has $x^TAx=-1$, and $\det A=-11$. **There is no positive-diagonal LDL factorization for the printed <matrix>.** The unrestricted LDL decomposition above is nevertheless valid.

= 7H
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Statistics}

= Solution
{parent=7H}

Let $\bar X=n^{-1}\sum_iX_i$ and $S=\sum_i(X_i-\bar X)^2$. The <Gaussian distribution> <likelihood> is proportional to $\tau^{n/2}\exp[-\tau\sum_i(X_i-\mu)^2/2]$. Multiplying by the <normal-gamma distribution> prior gives the quadratic exponent $\sum_i(X_i-\mu)^2+K_0(\mu-\mu_0)^2+2\beta_0$. Completing the square,
$$
\sum_i(X_i-\mu)^2+K_0(\mu-\mu_0)^2=(K_0+n)(\mu-\mu_n)^2+S+\frac{K_0n}{K_0+n}(\bar X-\mu_0)^2.
$$
The power of $\tau$ is $\tau^{\alpha_0+n/2-1}\sqrt\tau$, so the <posterior distribution> has exactly the required family with
$$
\boxed{K_n=K_0+n,\qquad\mu_n=\frac{K_0\mu_0+n\bar X}{K_0+n},\qquad\alpha_n=\alpha_0+\frac n2,}
$$
and
$$
\boxed{\beta_n=\beta_0+\frac12\sum_i(X_i-\bar X)^2+\frac{K_0n}{2(K_0+n)}(\bar X-\mu_0)^2.}
$$
For a proper prior, $K_0,\alpha_0,\beta_0>0$ and $\mu_0\in\mathbb R$. The separate $\sqrt\tau$ factor is retained in both densities; it does not add $1/2$ to the update of $\alpha$. This is a <conjugate prior> for the Gaussian mean and <precision parameter>.

= 8H
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Optimization}

= Solution
{parent=8H}

The <Lagrangian sufficiency theorem> for an equality constraint states that if a feasible $x_*$ and a multiplier $\lambda_*$ exist such that $x_*$ globally maximizes $L(x,\lambda_*)=f(x)-\lambda_*\cdot(g(x)-b)$ over the underlying domain, then $x_*$ globally maximizes $f$ over $g(x)=b$. Indeed $L=f$ at every feasible point, so global maximality of $L$ directly compares the feasible objective values. The underlying domain can already impose $x_i\geq0$.

For $0<p<1$, choose $\lambda=p d^{1-p}$. Each function $t^p-\lambda t$ on $t\geq0$ is a <strictly concave function> and attains its unique maximum at $t=1/d$, since $pt^{p-1}=\lambda$ there. The sum <Lagrangian> is therefore globally maximized when every $x_i=1/d$, and this point is feasible. For $p=1$ the objective is identically one on the feasible set. For $p>1$, each feasible $x_i$ lies in $[0,1]$, so $x_i^p\leq x_i$, with equality only at $0$ or $1$; equality in the total is attained exactly at a <simplex> vertex. Consequently
$$
\boxed{\max\sum_{i=1}^d x_i^p=\begin{cases}d^{1-p},&0<p<1,\\1,&p\geq1.\end{cases}}
$$
For $0<p<1$ the unique maximizer is $(1/d,\ldots,1/d)$; for $p=1$ every feasible point is a maximizer; for $p>1$ the maximizers are exactly the $d$ vertices with one coordinate one and all others zero. When $d=1$ these descriptions coincide.

= 9E
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Linear Algebra}

= Solution
{parent=9E}

The <row rank> of $A$ is the dimension of the span of its rows in $\mathbb R^n$; its <column rank> is the dimension of the span of its columns in $\mathbb R^m$. <Elementary row operations> replace rows by invertible <linear combinations>, so preserve their span. They also preserve every column relation: $Ac=0$ if and only if $EAc=0$ for an invertible <elementary matrix> $E$. Reduce $A$ to <row echelon form> $R$, with $r$ nonzero rows. These rows are independent because their leading entries occur in distinct, successively later columns. The $r$ pivot columns are independent by their triangular pivot submatrix, while every column lies in the $r$-dimensional coordinate space supported on the nonzero rows. Thus both ranks of $R$, and hence both ranks of $A$, equal $r$. **<Row rank> equals <column rank>.**

<Elementary column operations> replace columns by invertible <linear combinations> and preserve their span. Equivalently, right multiplication by an invertible <elementary matrix> $F$ preserves row relations, since $c^TAF=0$ if and only if $c^TA=0$. Therefore either <elementary row operations> or <elementary column operations> preserve the <matrix rank>, and any finite sequence gives $\boxed{\operatorname{rank}(A')=\operatorname{rank}(A)}$.

For the block <matrices>, put $M=\begin{pmatrix}PQ&0\\Q&QR\end{pmatrix}$. The explicit invertible multiplications
$$
\begin{pmatrix}I&-P\\0&I\end{pmatrix}M\begin{pmatrix}I&-R\\0&I\end{pmatrix}=\begin{pmatrix}0&-PQR\\Q&0\end{pmatrix}
$$
follow by first subtracting $P$ times the lower block row from the upper one and then subtracting the first block column times $R$ from the second. Changing the sign of the upper block row gives $N=\begin{pmatrix}0&PQR\\Q&0\end{pmatrix}$. These operations preserve <matrix rank>, proving that the two specified block <matrices> have equal rank. Their off-diagonal form gives $\operatorname{rank}(N)=\operatorname{rank}(Q)+\operatorname{rank}(PQR)$, since the two image components occupy independent coordinate blocks.

Choose $r_1=\operatorname{rank}(PQ)$ independent columns of $PQ$ and $r_2=\operatorname{rank}(QR)$ independent columns of $QR$. Select the corresponding $r_1$ columns from the first block column of $M$ and $r_2$ from the second. In a <linear relation> among these selected columns, the upper coordinate block forces every coefficient of the first selection to vanish; the lower block then forces every coefficient of the second to vanish. Consequently $\operatorname{rank}(M)\geq r_1+r_2$. Combining this with the equality of block ranks proves the <Frobenius rank inequality>:
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{rank}(PQ)+\operatorname{rank}(QR)\leq\operatorname{rank}(Q)+\operatorname{rank}(PQR).}
$$

= 10G
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Groups, Rings and Modules}

= i
{parent=10g}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The <alternating group> $A_4$ contains
$$
V=\{1,(12)(34),(13)(24),(14)(23)\}.
$$
The product of any two different nonidentity elements is the third, and each squares to the identity, so $V$ is a <Klein four-group>. Conjugation by a permutation replaces the labels in each cycle, preserving the property of being a product of two disjoint transpositions. Thus $V$ is a <normal subgroup> of $A_4$. Its order is $4$, strictly between $1$ and $|A_4|=12$. **$A_4$ is not simple.**

= ii
{parent=10g}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Write $G=\operatorname{Rot}(D)$ for the <rotational symmetry group of a regular dodecahedron>. Centre the solid at the origin. A <rotation in three dimensions> is determined by the image of a chosen face and one of its vertices, so there are at most $12\cdot5=60$ rotations. All these choices occur. The outward unit normal to a face and the direction from its centre to the chosen vertex determine an oriented orthonormal frame. The proper rotation mapping one such frame to another maps the whole regular pentagonal face. At each edge, the common dihedral angle and convexity then determine the adjacent face uniquely; propagating across the connected face-adjacency graph shows that the rotation maps the entire solid to itself. Hence $|G|=60$, and the action is transitive on faces, vertices and edges.

Its rotations can be counted geometrically. There are six axes joining opposite face centres, each allowing four nonidentity rotations of order five; ten axes joining opposite vertices, each allowing two of order three; and fifteen axes joining opposite edge midpoints, each allowing a half-turn. The <orbit-stabilizer theorem> also verifies these possibilities: vertex stabilizers have order $60/20=3$ and edge stabilizers have order $60/30=2$, and their nonidentity elements rotate about the corresponding vertex or edge-midpoint axis. Together with the identity this accounts for all $1+24+20+15=60$ elements. In particular there are no elements of order four.

We now determine the <conjugacy classes>. The group is transitive on edges, so all fifteen edge half-turns are conjugate. For a rotation whose angle is neither zero nor $\pi$, a commuting rotation must preserve its oriented axis: conjugation carries the axis and its rotation angle to their images, and reversing the axis would reverse a non-half-turn angle. Thus its <centralizer> consists of rotations about the same axis. For a face rotation this <centralizer> has order five, and for a vertex rotation it has order three. The <orbit-stabilizer theorem> for conjugation therefore gives class sizes $60/5=12$ and $60/3=20$. The twenty vertex rotations form one class, and the twenty-four face rotations form two classes. **The class sizes are $1,15,20,12,12$.**

A <normal subgroup> is a union of these classes and contains the identity. Its possible orders are
$$
1,13,16,21,25,28,33,36,40,45,48,60.
$$
By <Lagrange's theorem> its order must divide $60$. Only $1$ and $60$ in this list do so. Therefore $\boxed{G\text{ is simple of order }60}$.

= iii
{parent=10g}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

For each <involution> $t\in G$, its <conjugacy class> has size fifteen, so its <centralizer> $C_G(t)$ has order four. A group of order four is a <cyclic group> or a <Klein four-group>; the cyclic possibility would contain an element of order four, excluded by the geometric enumeration. Hence each $C_G(t)$ is a <Klein four-group> containing three <involutions>.

If $u$ is any of the three <involutions> in $C_G(t)$, the latter group is an <abelian group> and hence is contained in $C_G(u)$. Both <centralizers> have order four, so they are equal. It follows that the fifteen <involutions> partition into five triples, with five distinct <centralizers>. Conjugation by $G$ permutes these five subgroups and gives a <group homomorphism> $\theta:G\to S_5$. This action is nontrivial: otherwise each of these order-four subgroups would be a <normal subgroup>, contrary to simplicity. The <kernel> of $\theta$ is normal; since $G$ is simple and the action nontrivial, its <kernel> is trivial. Thus $\theta$ is injective.

The sign of $\theta(g)$ defines a homomorphism $G\to\{\pm1\}$. If it were nontrivial, simplicity would make it injective, impossible for a group of order sixty mapping into one of order two. Hence every image permutation is even, and $\theta(G)\subseteq A_5$. Both groups have order sixty, so they are equal. This constructs the requested <group isomorphism>:
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{Rot}(D)\cong A_5.}
$$

= 11F
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Analysis II}

= Solution
{parent=11F}

The <contraction mapping theorem> states that a <contraction mapping> $T:X\to X$ on a nonempty <complete metric space> has a unique <fixed point>. Here contraction means that $d(Tx,Ty)\leq qd(x,y)$ for all $x,y$, with one constant $0\leq q<1$. Choose $x_0\in X$ and define $x_{n+1}=Tx_n$. Successive distances satisfy $d(x_{n+1},x_n)\leq q^nd(x_1,x_0)$, so for every $m\geq1$,
$$
d(x_{n+m},x_n)\leq\sum_{j=n}^{n+m-1}q^jd(x_1,x_0)\leq\frac{q^n}{1-q}d(x_1,x_0).
$$
Thus $(x_n)$ is a <Cauchy sequence>. Completeness gives $x_n\to x_*$. Since a <contraction mapping> is a <continuous function>, $Tx_* =\lim Tx_n=\lim x_{n+1}=x_*$. If $y_*$ were another <fixed point>, $d(x_*,y_*)=d(Tx_*,Ty_*)\leq qd(x_*,y_*)$, forcing distance zero. This proves both existence and uniqueness.

Now suppose $T=f^k$ is a <contraction mapping>, where $k$ is a positive integer. Let $y$ be its unique <fixed point>. Since $f$ commutes with its own iterate, $T(fy)=f(Ty)=fy$. Thus $fy$ is also a <fixed point> of $T$, so $fy=y$. Any <fixed point> of $f$ is a <fixed point> of $T$, and must equal $y$. **A contractive iterate therefore gives a unique <fixed point> of $f$**, without assuming continuity of $f$ itself.

The space $X=C([0,1],\mathbb R)$ is complete for the <uniform norm>. Indeed, a uniformly <Cauchy sequence> converges pointwise by completeness of $\mathbb R$; passing to the limit in its uniform Cauchy bound shows <uniform convergence>, and the uniform limit of <continuous functions> is a <continuous function>. The operator $F$ maps this space to itself: $s\mapsto\phi(h(s),s)$ is a <continuous function>, and its integral plus the continuous $g$ is a <continuous function>.

For $n=0$ the desired bound is the definition of $\|h-k\|_\infty$. Suppose it holds for $n$. The <Lipschitz condition> and the fact that the same $g$ cancels give
$$
\begin{aligned}|F^{n+1}(h)(t)-F^{n+1}(k)(t)|&\leq\int_0^t M|F^n(h)(s)-F^n(k)(s)|\,ds\\&\leq\frac{M^{n+1}}{n!}\|h-k\|_\infty\int_0^t s^n\,ds\\&=\frac{M^{n+1}t^{n+1}}{(n+1)!}\|h-k\|_\infty.
\end{aligned}
$$
This proves the estimate by induction. Taking the supremum over $0\leq t\leq1$, the Lipschitz constant of $F^n$ is at most $M^n/n!$. These constants tend to zero, since the ratio of successive terms is $M/(n+1)$. Some iterate is consequently a contraction. The preceding result gives a unique <fixed point> of $F$, which is exactly $\boxed{\text{the unique continuous solution of the integral equation on }[0,1]}$. No assumption $M<1$ is needed.

= 12F
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Metric and Topological Spaces}

= Solution
{parent=12F}

A <topology> on $X$ is a family $\mathcal T$ of subsets containing $\varnothing$ and $X$, closed under arbitrary unions and finite intersections. Its members are the <open sets>. A <Hausdorff space> separates each pair of distinct points by disjoint open neighbourhoods; a <compact space> is one for which every <open cover> has a finite subcover.

= a
{parent=12f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

**Not a <topology>.** Each singleton $\{n\}$ with $n>0$ is finite and hence belongs to the proposed family, but their union $\{1,2,3,\ldots\}$ is infinite and excludes zero, so does not belong. The arbitrary-union axiom fails.

= b
{parent=12f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

**A <Hausdorff> compact <topology>.** The empty set omits zero and $\mathbb Z$ has finite complement. An arbitrary union omitting zero belongs to the family. If a union contains zero, one of its constituent sets contains zero and is cofinite, so the union is also cofinite. A finite intersection either omits zero or is an intersection of finitely many cofinite sets and is therefore cofinite. This proves all <topology> axioms.

For distinct nonzero integers $a,b$, the disjoint open singletons $\{a\},\{b\}$ separate them. To separate $0$ and $n\ne0$, use the disjoint <open sets> $\mathbb Z\setminus\{n\}$ and $\{n\}$. Thus this is a <Hausdorff space>. Every <open cover> has a member containing zero, which leaves only finitely many integers uncovered; choose one member for each of those points. This gives a finite subcover and proves <compactness>. The space is the <one-point compactification> of the <discrete space> $\mathbb Z\setminus\{0\}$, with zero as the added point.

= c
{parent=12f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

**Not a <topology>.** For every integer $k\geq2$, the set $U_k=\mathbb Z\setminus k\mathbb Z$ is periodic with period $k$, so belongs to the family. Their union is $\mathbb Z\setminus\{0\}$: for a nonzero $n$, choose $k>|n|$, which cannot divide $n$. But this union has no positive integer period. If it had period $k$, membership of $-k$ would force membership of zero. Again arbitrary unions fail the <topology> axiom.

= d
{parent=12f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

**A <Hausdorff>, noncompact <topology>.** The condition says precisely that each point of $U$ lies in a <residue class> $n+k\mathbb Z$ contained in $U$. These classes form a basis: the intersection of two classes containing a common point contains the class through that point with modulus equal to the least common multiple of their moduli. Thus arbitrary unions obey the condition, and so do finite intersections. Also $\varnothing$ and $\mathbb Z$ obey it. This is the <profinite topology> on $\mathbb Z$.

If $a\ne b$, choose $k>|a-b|$. Then $a+k\mathbb Z$ and $b+k\mathbb Z$ are disjoint open neighbourhoods, proving the <Hausdorff> property. To test <compactness>, define
$$
a_n=\frac{4^n-1}{3},\qquad F_n=a_n+4^n\mathbb Z\quad(n\geq1).
$$
Every $F_n$ is nonempty and both open and closed: its complement is a finite union of other <residue classes>. Since $a_{n+1}-a_n=4^n$, $F_{n+1}\subseteq F_n$. If $x$ belonged to their intersection, then $3x+1$ would be divisible by $4^n$ for every $n$, forcing $3x+1=0$, impossible for an integer. Therefore $U_n=\mathbb Z\setminus F_n$ is an increasing <open cover> of $\mathbb Z$. Any finite subcollection has union $U_N$ for its largest index and misses the nonempty $F_N$. This proves that the <profinite topology on the integers is not compact>.

= 13C
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Complex Analysis or Complex Methods}

= a
{parent=13c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Put $a=1/n$, so $0<a<1$, and write $I(a)=\int_0^\infty x^a/(1+x^2)\,dx$. Choose the <branch of the complex logarithm> with $-\pi/2<\arg z<3\pi/2$, whose cut is the downward imaginary axis. Define $z^a=\exp(a\operatorname{Log}z)$ and integrate $z^a/(1+z^2)$ along the positively oriented upper semicircle, indented above zero. The straight pieces run from $-R$ to $-\varepsilon$ and from $\varepsilon$ to $R$; the small arc runs clockwise from argument $\pi$ to zero.

On the negative real axis $z^a=|z|^ae^{i\pi a}$, so the two straight pieces tend to $(1+e^{i\pi a})I(a)$. The outer arc is $O(R^{a-1})$ and the inner arc is $O(\varepsilon^{a+1})$, both tending to zero. The only enclosed <pole> is $i$, with <residue> $e^{i\pi a/2}/(2i)$. The <residue theorem> therefore gives
$$
(1+e^{i\pi a})I(a)=\pi e^{i\pi a/2}.
$$
Using $1+e^{i\pi a}=2e^{i\pi a/2}\cos(\pi a/2)$ yields
$$
\boxed{I(1/n)=\frac{\pi}{2\cos(\pi/(2n))}.}
$$
The same bounds show convergence of the real integral at both zero and infinity.

= b
{parent=13c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Use the same indented upper-semicircle contour and <branch of the complex logarithm>, now for $F(z)=z^{1/2}\operatorname{Log}z/(1+z^2)$. Write $J=\int_0^\infty x^{1/2}\log x/(1+x^2)\,dx$. On the negative axis, $z^{1/2}=i|z|^{1/2}$ and $\operatorname{Log}z=\log|z|+i\pi$. Consequently the two straight contributions tend to
$$
(1+i)J-\pi I(1/2).
$$
The outer arc is $O(R^{-1/2}\log R)$ and the small arc is $O(\varepsilon^{3/2}(|\log\varepsilon|+1))$, so neither contributes in the limit. At the enclosed <pole> $i$,
$$
\operatorname{Res}_{z=i}F=\frac{e^{i\pi/4}(i\pi/2)}{2i}=\frac\pi4e^{i\pi/4}.
$$
The <residue theorem> gives $(1+i)J-\pi I(1/2)=i\pi^2e^{i\pi/4}/2$. Part (a) gives $I(1/2)=\pi/\sqrt2$. Substituting and using $e^{i\pi/4}=(1+i)/\sqrt2$, the right side after moving the $I$ term is $\pi^2(1+i)/(2\sqrt2)$. Hence
$$
\boxed{J=\frac{\pi^2}{2\sqrt2}.}
$$

= 14D
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Methods}

= Solution
{parent=14D}

For variations $y+\epsilon\eta$ with $\eta(x_1)=\eta(x_2)=0$, <integration by parts> gives $\delta I=\int_{x_1}^{x_2}[f_y-(d/dx)f_{y'}]\eta\,dx$. Since this vanishes for every such variation, the <Euler-Lagrange equation> is
$$
\boxed{\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}-\frac{d}{dx}\frac{\partial f}{\partial y'}=0.}
$$
When $f$ is independent of $y$, it has the <first integral> $\boxed{\partial f/\partial y'=C}$.

For a ray represented as a graph traversed with increasing $x$, the travel time is $T=\int_{x_1}^{x_2}\sqrt{1+y'^2}/c(x)\,dx$, with $c(x)>0$. By <Fermat principle>, use $f=\sqrt{1+y'^2}/c(x)$. The <first integral> becomes
$$
\frac{y'}{c(x)\sqrt{1+y'^2}}=C.
$$
The sign of $y'$ is constant, and $y_2>y_1$ makes it positive. Write $C=1/k$ with $k>0$ and solve to obtain
$$
\boxed{y'=\frac{c(x)}{\sqrt{k^2-c(x)^2}},\qquad y_2-y_1=\int_{x_1}^{x_2}\frac{c(x)}{\sqrt{k^2-c(x)^2}}\,dx.}
$$
A finite slope requires $k>c(x)$ in the interior; a limiting vertical tangent is possible at an endpoint.

For $c(x)=ax$ and $k=ax_2$, positive speed on the whole interval and $k\geq c(x)$ select $a>0$ and $0<x_1<x_2$. The slope is $x/\sqrt{x_2^2-x^2}$. Integrating backwards from $(x_2,y_2)$ gives
$$
y_2-y=\int_x^{x_2}\frac{u}{\sqrt{x_2^2-u^2}}\,du=\sqrt{x_2^2-x^2},
$$
so $\boxed{(y_2-y)^2=x_2^2-x^2}$, with the branch $y\leq y_2$. The endpoint integral is finite despite the vertical tangent. The specified value $k=ax_2$ fits the first endpoint precisely when $y_2-y_1=\sqrt{x_2^2-x_1^2}$; for other endpoint data the preceding integral determines a different $k$.

= 15A
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Quantum Mechanics}

= Solution
{parent=15A}

The <time-independent Schrodinger equation> is $[-\hbar^2\nabla^2/(2m)+V]\Psi=E\Psi$. For the <hydrogen atom>, separate variables as $\Psi=g(r)Y_{\ell m}(\theta,\varphi)$. The spherical-coordinate <Laplacian> has radial part $r^{-2}\partial_r(r^2\partial_r)$ and angular part $r^{-2}\Delta_{S^2}$. A <spherical harmonic> obeys $\Delta_{S^2}Y_{\ell m}=-\ell(\ell+1)Y_{\ell m}$. The first displayed term is therefore radial kinetic energy; the second is the attractive <Coulomb's law> potential energy $-e^2/(4\pi\varepsilon_0r)$ of electron and proton; the third is the angular kinetic energy, also called the <centrifugal potential>. Regular single-valued angular states have $\boxed{\ell=0,1,2,\ldots}$, with <orbital angular momentum> squared $\hbar^2\ell(\ell+1)$.

For $g=r^\alpha e^{-\beta r}$, direct differentiation gives
$$
\frac{g''+2g'/r}{g}=\frac{\alpha(\alpha+1)}{r^2}-\frac{2\beta(\alpha+1)}r+\beta^2.
$$
Substitution into the <radial Schrodinger equation for the hydrogen atom> and equality of the coefficients of $r^{-2}$, $r^{-1}$ and the constant term give
$$
\alpha(\alpha+1)=\ell(\ell+1),\qquad \frac{\hbar^2\beta(\alpha+1)}m=\frac{e^2}{4\pi\varepsilon_0},\qquad E=-\frac{\hbar^2\beta^2}{2m}.
$$
The regular radial solution selects $\alpha=\ell$, rather than the singular branch $-\ell-1$. At $\ell=0$, $r^{-1}$ is locally square integrable but is still inadmissible as a regular Coulomb eigenfunction: its <Laplacian> has a point-source contribution at the origin. Bound-state decay requires $\beta>0$. Thus, with the <Bohr radius> $a_0=4\pi\varepsilon_0\hbar^2/(me^2)$,
$$
\boxed{\alpha=\ell,\qquad\beta=\frac1{a_0(\ell+1)},\qquad E_\ell=-\frac{me^4}{2(4\pi\varepsilon_0)^2\hbar^2(\ell+1)^2}.}
$$
These are the nodeless states with <principal quantum number> $n=\ell+1$. Increasing $\ell$ raises the energy towards zero, so emission is from the state labelled $\ell+1$ to the one labelled $\ell$. Using the <Planck constant> $h=2\pi\hbar$ and <photon> energy $h\nu$, the frequency is
$$
\boxed{\nu=\frac{me^4}{2(4\pi\varepsilon_0)^2\hbar^2h}\left[\frac1{(\ell+1)^2}-\frac1{(\ell+2)^2}\right].}
$$

= 16B
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Electromagnetism}

= i
{parent=16b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Charge conservation gives the <continuity equation> $\partial_t\rho+\nabla\cdot\mathbf J=0$. Since $\mathbf J$ is independent of time, so is $\partial_t\rho=-\nabla\cdot\mathbf J$. Integrating at each fixed position gives
$$
\boxed{\rho(\mathbf r,t)=\rho(\mathbf r,0)-t\nabla\cdot\mathbf J(\mathbf r)=\rho(\mathbf r,0)+t\dot\rho(\mathbf r,0).}
$$

= ii
{parent=16b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Let $s=|\mathbf r-\mathbf r'|$ and introduce the <magnetic vector potential> $\mathbf A(\mathbf r)=\mu_0(4\pi)^{-1}\int\mathbf J(\mathbf r')/s\,dV'$. Differentiation is with respect to $\mathbf r$, so $\mathbf J(\mathbf r')$ is constant under it. Hence
$$
\nabla\times\frac{\mathbf J(\mathbf r')}{s}=\nabla(s^{-1})\times\mathbf J(\mathbf r')=-\frac{\mathbf r-\mathbf r'}{s^3}\times\mathbf J(\mathbf r')=\frac{\mathbf J(\mathbf r')\times(\mathbf r-\mathbf r')}{s^3}.
$$
Taking the curl through the integral reproduces the <Biot-Savart law>, proving $\boxed{\mathbf B=\nabla\times\mathbf A}$ and the requested representation.

= iii
{parent=16b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Use the identity $\nabla\times\mathbf B=\nabla(\nabla\cdot\mathbf A)-\nabla^2\mathbf A$. The <Laplacian> identity for the Coulomb <kernel> gives, component by component,
$$
-\nabla^2\mathbf A=-\frac{\mu_0}{4\pi}\int\mathbf J(\mathbf r')\nabla^2(s^{-1})\,dV'=\mu_0\mathbf J(\mathbf r).
$$
Also $\nabla(s^{-1})=-\nabla'(s^{-1})$, so <integration by parts> yields
$$
\begin{aligned}\nabla\cdot\mathbf A&=-\frac{\mu_0}{4\pi}\int\mathbf J(\mathbf r')\cdot\nabla'(s^{-1})\,dV'\\&=\frac{\mu_0}{4\pi}\int\frac{\nabla'\cdot\mathbf J(\mathbf r')}{s}\,dV'.\end{aligned}
$$
Here the surface integral at infinity vanishes under the assumed localization of the current. As usual, this assumption means sufficient decay for these integrals and the boundary limit, rather than merely pointwise convergence to zero. Combining the two terms gives
$$
\boxed{\nabla\times\mathbf B=\mu_0\mathbf J+\frac{\mu_0}{4\pi}\nabla\int\frac{\nabla'\cdot\mathbf J(\mathbf r')}{|\mathbf r-\mathbf r'|}\,dV'.}
$$

= iv
{parent=16b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

For the charge-generated <electric field>, use the <electric potential>
$$
\phi(\mathbf r,t)=\frac1{4\pi\varepsilon_0}\int\frac{\rho(\mathbf r',t)}{|\mathbf r-\mathbf r'|}\,dV',\qquad\mathbf E=-\nabla\phi.
$$
The <vector> potential is time independent, so its usual additional contribution $-\partial_t\mathbf A$ is zero. The <continuity equation> now gives
$$
\partial_t\mathbf E=-\frac1{4\pi\varepsilon_0}\nabla\int\frac{\dot\rho(\mathbf r',t)}s\,dV'=\frac1{4\pi\varepsilon_0}\nabla\int\frac{\nabla'\cdot\mathbf J(\mathbf r')}s\,dV'.
$$
Thus the second term in part (iii) is exactly the <displacement current> contribution $\mu_0\varepsilon_0\partial_t\mathbf E$, and
$$
\boxed{\nabla\times\mathbf B=\mu_0\mathbf J+\mu_0\varepsilon_0\partial_t\mathbf E.}
$$
This is the <Ampère-Maxwell equation>. The constructed field also has zero curl, consistent with the time-independent <magnetic field> and <Faraday's law>. A static addition to the <electric field> does not change this conclusion; the integral construction selects the fields sourced by the given densities rather than an independently added radiation field.

= 17B
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Fluid Dynamics}

= Solution
{parent=17B}

Distinguish the full <velocity potentials> $\Phi_i=U_ix+\varphi_i$ from their perturbations $\varphi_i$. The linear <boundary conditions> use the perturbations. This distinction reconciles the notation in the printed conditions with the full potentials written in the mode ansatz. All equations below neglect products of perturbations. Assume positive densities and no <surface tension>.

= i
{parent=17b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The interface is a material surface, so its defining function $F=z-\zeta(x,y,t)$ obeys $DF/Dt=0$ on each side. Since $\mathbf u_i=\nabla\Phi_i$, the exact <kinematic boundary condition> is
$$
\partial_z\Phi_i=\partial_t\zeta+(\partial_x\Phi_i)\partial_x\zeta+(\partial_y\Phi_i)\partial_y\zeta\qquad(z=\zeta).
$$
Substitute $\Phi_i=U_ix+\varphi_i$. Products such as $\varphi_{ix}\zeta_x$ are second order, as is the correction from evaluating $\varphi_{iz}$ at $z=\zeta$ instead of zero. Linearization therefore gives
$$
\boxed{\partial_z\varphi_i=\partial_t\zeta+U_i\partial_x\zeta\qquad(z=0,\ i=1,2).}
$$

= ii
{parent=17b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Without <surface tension>, the exact <dynamic boundary condition for an inviscid interface> is equality of pressure, $p_1=p_2$, at $z=\zeta$. The <unsteady Bernoulli equation> in each irrotational fluid is
$$
\partial_t\Phi_i+\tfrac12|\nabla\Phi_i|^2+p_i/\rho_i+gz=C_i(t).
$$
Choose the potential gauge so that $C_i=p_0/\rho_i+U_i^2/2$, its unperturbed value. Expanding the kinetic term as $U_i^2/2+U_i\partial_x\varphi_i$ and evaluating gravity at the displaced interface gives
$$
p_i|_{z=\zeta}=p_0-\rho_i[\partial_t\varphi_i+U_i\partial_x\varphi_i+g\zeta]
$$
to first order. Equality of these pressures gives
$$
\boxed{\rho_1[(\partial_t+U_1\partial_x)\varphi_1+g\zeta]=\rho_2[(\partial_t+U_2\partial_x)\varphi_2+g\zeta]\qquad(z=0).}
$$

= iii
{parent=17b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

<Incompressibility> makes each perturbation <velocity potential> a <harmonic function>. A horizontal mode $e^{i(kx+\ell y)+st}$ has $q=\sqrt{k^2+\ell^2}>0$. Decay away from the interface selects $\varphi_1=A_1e^{qz}e^{i(kx+\ell y)+st}$ below and $\varphi_2=A_2e^{-qz}e^{i(kx+\ell y)+st}$ above. The <kinematic boundary condition> gives
$$
qA_1=(s+ikU_1)\zeta_0,\qquad-qA_2=(s+ikU_2)\zeta_0.
$$
Insert these into the <dynamic boundary condition for an inviscid interface> and cancel a nonzero mode amplitude to obtain
$$
\rho_1(s+ikU_1)^2+\rho_2(s+ikU_2)^2+qg(\rho_1-\rho_2)=0.
$$
Writing $\rho_T=\rho_1+\rho_2$ and $\bar U=(\rho_1U_1+\rho_2U_2)/\rho_T$, completion of the square gives
$$
\rho_T(s+ik\bar U)^2-\frac{k^2\rho_1\rho_2}{\rho_T}(U_1-U_2)^2+qg(\rho_1-\rho_2)=0.
$$
Consequently
$$
\boxed{s=-ik\frac{\rho_1U_1+\rho_2U_2}{\rho_1+\rho_2}\ \pm\sqrt{\frac{k^2\rho_1\rho_2(U_1-U_2)^2}{(\rho_1+\rho_2)^2}-\frac{qg(\rho_1-\rho_2)}{\rho_1+\rho_2}}.}
$$
The imaginary advection term does not change growth rates. A mode grows exponentially precisely when the radicand is positive, equivalently
$$
\boxed{k^2\rho_1\rho_2(U_1-U_2)^2>qg(\rho_1^2-\rho_2^2).}
$$
If the upper fluid is heavier, every nonzero wavevector is unstable even without shear: this is <Rayleigh-Taylor instability>. If the lower fluid is heavier, gravity stabilizes sufficiently long waves, but any nonzero velocity difference destabilizes sufficiently short waves with $\ell=0$: this is <Kelvin-Helmholtz instability>. For equal densities, shear destabilizes modes with $k\ne0$. A negative radicand gives purely imaginary frequencies and oscillatory modes; equality is the marginal threshold for exponential growth.

= 18H
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Statistics}

= i
{parent=18h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Write $\bar X=n^{-1}\sum_iX_i$ and $\bar Y=n^{-1}\sum_iY_i$. Completing the square in each sample, the log <likelihood> is, up to a data-dependent constant,
$$
\ell(\mu_X,\mu_Y)=-\frac n2[(\mu_X-\bar X)^2+(\mu_Y-\bar Y)^2].
$$
The <maximum-likelihood estimators> follow because it is strictly maximized over unrestricted means at $(\bar X,\bar Y)$. Under the null it is maximized at $(\bar X,0)$. Thus the <likelihood ratio> is $\Lambda=\exp(-n\bar Y^2/2)$, and the <likelihood-ratio test> rejects for large $T=-2\log\Lambda=n\bar Y^2$.

Under every null value of $\mu_X$, $Z=\sqrt n\bar Y$ has the standard <Gaussian distribution>; hence $T$ has the <chi-squared distribution> with one degree of freedom. Let $z_p=\Phi^{-1}(p)$, where $\Phi$ is the standard Gaussian <cumulative distribution function>. The exact size-$\alpha$ test is
$$
\boxed{\text{reject if }|\sqrt n\bar Y|>z_{1-\alpha/2}.}
$$
The null rejection probability is $2[1-\Phi(z_{1-\alpha/2})]=\alpha$, independently of the nuisance mean $\mu_X$. No randomization is required because the statistic has a continuous distribution.

= ii
{parent=18h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The unrestricted <maximum-likelihood estimators> remain $(\bar X,\bar Y)$. Subject to the null constraint, minimizing $(\mu_X-\bar X)^2$ on $[A,\infty)$ gives $\widehat\mu_X=\max(A,\bar X)$, while $\widehat\mu_Y=0$. Consequently
$$
\boxed{T=-2\log\Lambda=n[\bar Y^2+(A-\bar X)_+^2],\qquad \text{reject if }T>c_\alpha.}
$$
We must calibrate this <likelihood-ratio test> over the entire composite null. Under that null let $W=\sqrt n(\bar X-A)$ and $Z=\sqrt n\bar Y$. Then $W\sim N(\delta,1)$ with $\delta=\sqrt n(\mu_X-A)\geq0$, while $Z\sim N(0,1)$ independently, and $T=Z^2+(-W)_+^2$. Couple all null distributions by $W=W_0+\delta$, with a fixed standard Gaussian $W_0$. Increasing $\delta$ decreases $(-W_0-\delta)_+^2$ pointwise, so the largest rejection probability occurs at $\delta=0$, namely $\mu_X=A$.

At this boundary the sign of $W$ is independent of $W^2$, with each sign having probability one half. On $W\geq0$, $T=Z^2\sim\chi_1^2$; on $W<0$, $T=Z^2+W^2\sim\chi_2^2$. Thus the least favourable <null hypothesis> law is the exact <chi-bar-squared distribution> $\tfrac12\chi_1^2+\tfrac12\chi_2^2$. Choose $c_\alpha>0$ by
$$
\boxed{\frac12\mathbb P(\chi_1^2>c_\alpha)+\frac12\mathbb P(\chi_2^2>c_\alpha)=\alpha,\quad\text{equivalently }1-\Phi(\sqrt{c_\alpha})+\frac12e^{-c_\alpha/2}=\alpha.}
$$
The tail is a <continuous function> and strictly decreasing from one to zero, so this threshold is unique for $0<\alpha<1$. The null rejection probability is at most $\alpha$ and equals $\alpha$ at $\mu_X=A$. This proves the required size, rather than using an interior chi-squared approximation.

To compare, put $c_1=z_{1-\alpha/2}^2$, the threshold in (i). Since $\chi_2^2$ is stochastically larger than $\chi_1^2$ (add an independent squared standard Gaussian), the mixture threshold obeys $c_\alpha>c_1$. When $\bar X\geq A$, test (ii) therefore requires stronger evidence from $\bar Y$ than test (i). When $\bar X<A$, test (ii) also responds to violation of the mean constraint and can reject even with $\bar Y=0$. **Neither critical region contains the other.** In the coordinates $(W,Z)$, the acceptance region in (i) is the strip $|Z|\leq\sqrt{c_1}$; in (ii) it is the right half-strip $W\geq0$, $|Z|\leq\sqrt{c_\alpha}$ together with the left half-disk $W<0$, $W^2+Z^2\leq c_\alpha$. The extra constraint changes both the statistic and its correct null calibration.

= 19H
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Markov Chains}

= Solution
{parent=19H}

Let $L$ be the last vertex visited for the first time, and assume $N\geq2$. Every vertex is eventually painted with probability one: from any position, $N-1$ consecutive clockwise steps visit the entire cycle, and the probability of such a block is $2^{-(N-1)}$. Thus the probability that coverage has not occurred after $m$ successive blocks is at most $(1-2^{-(N-1)})^m$, which tends to zero.

Fix $j\ne0$ and cut the cycle at $j$. Represent it as the path with positions $0,1,\ldots,N$, where the two endpoints both represent $j$ and the original starting vertex is at position $N-j$. Up to the first visit to $j$, the walk is a <simple symmetric random walk> on this path, killed at either endpoint. The event $L=j$ is precisely that it visits both $1$ and $N-1$ before being killed: travelling between these two positions visits all intermediate vertices, while visiting all non-$j$ vertices necessarily includes these two.

For $N\geq3$, first wait until the walk reaches one of $1,N-1$. This happens almost surely before it can reach an absorbing endpoint. If it reaches $1$ first, it must next reach $N-1$ before zero. The <gambler's ruin> probability is $1/(N-1)$: the hitting probability $h(i)$ on $0\leq i\leq N-1$ satisfies
$$
h(0)=0,\quad h(N-1)=1,\quad h(i)=\tfrac12[h(i-1)+h(i+1)],
$$
so its successive differences are constant and $h(i)=i/(N-1)$. Starting at $1$ gives the stated probability. If the first endpoint reached is $N-1$, reflection of the path gives the same probability of reaching $1$ before $N$. The <strong Markov property> at the first such hit therefore gives $\mathbb P(L=j)=1/(N-1)$, independently of which endpoint was hit first and of the start position.

Consequently the <last visited vertex of a simple random walk on a cycle> has distribution
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(L=0)=0,\qquad\mathbb P(L=j)=\frac1{N-1}\quad(1\leq j\leq N-1).}
$$
For $N=2$ the same formula follows directly since the only other post must be last. For $N=1$, painting the initial post already completes the task, so there is no subsequent last unpainted post.
